================================================================================
 ${instance_name} - operator connection guide (OCI)
================================================================================

Region              : ${region}
Jump instance OCID  : ${instance_id}
Jump instance IP    : ${instance_private_ip}   (private - no public IP)
Bastion OCID        : ${bastion_id}
Bastion endpoint IP : ${bastion_endpoint_ip}
Max session TTL     : ${session_ttl} seconds
Migration mode      : ${migration_mode}

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
READ THIS FIRST - THIS IS NOT `aws ssm start-session`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
On AWS this host was reached with SSM Session Manager: no SSH key, no inbound
port, no local agent beyond the AWS CLI. OCI Bastion is a session BROKER for
SSH, not a replacement transport. Three things are different and none of them
is cosmetic:

  1. YOU NEED AN SSH KEYPAIR. Every OCI Bastion session requires one - the
     public half is registered with the session at creation time. There is no
     keyless mode.
  2. THIS HOST HAS ONE INBOUND RULE. Traffic from ${bastion_endpoint_ip} on
     port 22 is allowed. That rule cannot be removed; OCI Bastion cannot work
     without it, unlike SSM which needed zero inbound rules.
  3. NOTHING RECORDS WHAT YOU TYPE. OCI Audit logs that a session was created
     and destroyed, by whom and against what. It does not record session
     content. Self-managed recording is ${session_recording ? "ENABLED - a bucket exists, but the recorder itself is platform-built tooling; confirm it is actually running before relying on it" : "DISABLED"}.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 1 - one-time: have an SSH keypair
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/oci-bastion -C "$(whoami)@bastion"

The public half must already be authorized on this host (var.ssh_public_keys),
AND is passed again to each session below. Both are required.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 2 - create a PORT FORWARDING session to this host's sshd
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Port forwarding, not managed SSH. Managed SSH exists to reach arbitrary
instances without standing SSH access configured on them; this box's whole
purpose is being the one hop you tunnel THROUGH, so port forwarding is the
right type.

    oci bastion session create-port-forwarding \
      --bastion-id ${bastion_id} \
      --target-private-ip ${instance_private_ip} \
      --target-port 22 \
      --session-ttl ${session_ttl} \
      --ssh-public-key-file ~/.ssh/oci-bastion.pub \
      --region ${region} \
      --wait-for-state SUCCEEDED

Take the session OCID from the output, then:

    oci bastion session get --session-id <SESSION_OCID> --region ${region} \
      --query 'data."ssh-metadata".command'

That prints the exact ssh command, including the bastion host to proxy
through. Substitute your private key path for the <privateKey> placeholder.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 3 - tunnel onward to the data tier
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This host exists because the managed data-tier services do not run any agent a
broker can reach, so they cannot be targeted directly. Open a local forward
through the SSH connection from step 2:
%{ if length(connection_targets) == 0 }
  (no connection targets were wired into this module - the live root populates
   them from the database/cache units via dependency blocks)
%{ else }%{ for name, t in connection_targets }
  ${name}${t.description != "" ? " - ${t.description}" : ""}
      ssh -i ~/.ssh/oci-bastion \
          -o ProxyCommand="..." \
          -N -L ${t.port}:${t.host}:${t.port} \
          opc@${instance_private_ip}

      then connect to localhost:${t.port}
%{ endfor }%{ endif }
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT TO DO WHEN YOU ARE DONE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    oci bastion session delete --session-id <SESSION_OCID> --region ${region}

Sessions expire on their own after ${session_ttl} seconds, but that is a
LIFETIME cap, not an idle timer - an unused session stays open until it
expires. There is no OCI equivalent of SSM's idleSessionTimeout.
================================================================================
